<!-- §01 -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Measurement Problem</div>
<h2>Same Method, Three Different Speedups</h2>

<p>Consider DFlash, a new speculative decoding method that uses a diffusion-based draft model. Run it on GSM8K math problems with Qwen3-8B at temperature 0, batch size 1, and you get <strong>6.08×</strong> speedup. Swap to MT-Bench conversation prompts under identical conditions and you get <strong>2.75×</strong>. Move to batch size 64 for throughput measurement and the number changes again.</p>

<p>None of these numbers is wrong. They are all honest measurements of the same method. The problem is that a single speedup figure is meaningless without knowing which slice of the five-dimensional space it lives in:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">same-method-different-task.bench</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">same model · same GPU · same backend</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-problem" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The five variables that determine observed speedup are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>τ (mean accept length)</strong> — how many draft tokens the target accepts per cycle, determined by task and draft quality</li>
  <li><strong>c (draft cost ratio)</strong> — draft latency / verify latency, an architectural property of the method</li>
  <li><strong>γ (speculation budget)</strong> — maximum draft tokens per cycle, a hyperparameter</li>
  <li><strong>Batch size</strong> — latency mode (batch=1) vs throughput mode (batch=64+)</li>
  <li><strong>Backend framework</strong> — Transformers, SGLang, vLLM, TRT-LLM each give different absolute numbers</li>
</ul>

<p>A fair benchmark controls all five and reports at least three. In practice, most papers report one, picked to maximize the headline number.</p>

<div class="callout callout-amber">
  <strong>Why this matters:</strong> If you are choosing a speculative decoding method for a production LLM serving system, the number you care about is the speedup at your actual batch size, on your task distribution, in your inference framework — not the number in a paper's abstract.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §02 -->
<section id="s-formula" data-code="formula">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Speedup Formula</div>
<h2>Two Levers: Token Quality and Draft Cost</h2>

<p>Leviathan et al. (2023) derived a closed-form expression for speculative decoding speedup. Under the assumption of independent acceptance events with rate α, the expected number of tokens generated per cycle is <span style="font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--cyan)">τ = (1 − α^(γ+1)) / (1 − α)</span>. The wall-clock speedup relative to autoregressive decoding is then:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Speedup Formula</div>
  S = <span class="math-var">τ</span> / (1 + <span class="math-var">γ</span> · <span class="math-var">c</span>)
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">where τ = mean accepted tokens per cycle, γ = speculation budget, c = draft latency / verify latency</span>
</div>

<p>This formula exposes two independent levers. <strong>τ</strong> measures draft quality — how well the draft distribution matches the target. <strong>c</strong> measures draft efficiency — how cheap the drafting is relative to verification. A method can win by improving either lever, and the two are largely orthogonal.</p>

<p>The interactive visualization below lets you see how speedup responds to each lever. The labeled dots show where EAGLE-3 and DFlash sit on real Qwen3-8B measurements (γ=16, A100 GPU).</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">speedup-formula-explorer</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag sliders to explore</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <span class="ctrl-label">τ (accept length)</span>
      <input type="range" id="sl-tau" class="ctrl-slider" min="1" max="9" step="0.1" value="4.5">
      <span class="ctrl-val" id="sv-tau">4.5</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <span class="ctrl-label">c (cost ratio)</span>
      <input type="range" id="sl-c" class="ctrl-slider" min="0.01" max="0.45" step="0.01" value="0.17">
      <span class="ctrl-val" id="sv-c">0.17</span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-formula" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;margin-top:.5rem;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The two labeled points reveal why DFlash outpaces EAGLE-3 on the same model. EAGLE-3 achieves τ ≈ 3.5 (draft quality limited by its autoregressive single-layer architecture) but has c ≈ 0.17 (5.9 ms drafting per step). DFlash achieves τ ≈ 7.9 (parallel diffusion drafting captures more future tokens) with c ≈ 0.02 (0.8 ms drafting, a 7× cost reduction). Both levers move in DFlash's favor simultaneously.</p>

<div class="callout callout-cyan">
  <strong>Key insight:</strong> When a new method claims "we improved τ by 40%," ask how c changed. A 40% τ gain with a 3× c increase could net a lower actual speedup than before.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §03 -->
<section id="s-tasks" data-code="tasks">
<div class="section-no">03 — Task Sensitivity</div>
<h2>Math Tokens Are Easy to Predict; Chat Tokens Are Not</h2>

<p>The acceptance rate α is not a property of the method alone — it is a property of the method <em>on a specific task</em>. Math and code responses contain long runs of highly predictable tokens: digit sequences, operator patterns, import statements, variable names. Conversational responses are far more diverse. The same draft model sees radically different α values depending on what it is drafting.</p>

<p>The chart below shows mean acceptance length τ for EAGLE-3 (tree=60) and DFlash (block=16) across eight tasks on Qwen3-8B at temperature 0. The bars animate as you select each method.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">τ-by-task — Qwen3-8B, T=0, A100</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to switch method</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="task-btns" id="task-btns">
      <button class="task-btn active" data-method="eagle3">EAGLE-3 (tree=60)</button>
      <button class="task-btn" data-method="dflash">DFlash (block=16)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-tasks" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block;"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>EAGLE-3's τ ranges from 2.65 (LiveCodeBench) to 3.71 (GSM8K) — a 1.4× spread across tasks. DFlash's τ ranges from 4.24 (MT-Bench) to 7.87 (Math500) — a nearly 2× spread. Both methods are more sensitive to task than to most hyperparameters.</p>

<p>This means:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Reporting a single speedup number implicitly embeds a task distribution.</strong> A coding assistant sees very different speedup than a general-purpose chatbot.</li>
  <li><strong>LiveCodeBench is the hardest task for both methods</strong> — actual competitive programming outputs are diverse and hard to predict.</li>
  <li><strong>GSM8K and Math500 consistently give the highest τ</strong> — they are the most favorable tasks to report. Methods that only evaluate on these are selecting the best slice.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Spec-Bench's Six-Task Standard</h3>
<p>The Spec-Bench framework (Xia et al., 2024) defines a minimal set of six tasks that span conversational, code, math, summarization, and Q&amp;A categories: MT-Bench, HumanEval, GSM8K, Alpaca, CNN/DailyMail, and Natural Questions. Reporting across all six prevents cherry-picking and reveals the method's true distribution. Any benchmark that reports fewer than three task categories should be treated as a partial measurement.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 -->
<section id="s-batch" data-code="batch">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Batch Size Trap</div>
<h2>Speculative Decoding Is a Latency Optimization — Not a Throughput One</h2>

<p>Speculative decoding works by converting sequential token generation into batched verification. At batch size 1, the GPU sits mostly idle waiting for memory loads — speculative decoding fills that idle time productively. At large batch sizes, the GPU is already compute-bound; adding more work via speculative drafting adds overhead without proportional benefit.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">speedup-vs-batch-size — LLaMA-3.1 8B / SGLang / A100</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">throughput relative to autoregressive baseline</span>
  </div>
