<p>EAGLE at batch=64 is effectively a <em>slowdown</em> — the draft overhead exceeds the verification savings. EAGLE-3 recovers to 1.38× at batch=64 because its training-time test produces higher τ, meaning more tokens pass verification per drafting overhead paid. But it is still far from its 5.58× single-request speedup.</p>

<div class="callout callout-amber">
  <strong>Benchmark checklist:</strong> If the deployment target is a serving system handling concurrent requests, the relevant metric is throughput at realistic batch sizes — not latency at batch=1. Always report both, or the headline number describes a system that does not exist in production.
</div>

<p>This also means that the comparison between methods changes at different batch sizes. A method with slightly lower τ but cheaper drafting may win at batch=1 while losing at batch=8. A method with higher τ may become viable only at batch=1. Measuring at multiple batch sizes reveals which method wins for your actual deployment.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 -->
<section id="s-cost" data-code="cost">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Draft Cost Race</div>
<h2>The Hidden Variable: How Long Does Drafting Take?</h2>

<p>The c ratio (draft latency / verify latency) rarely appears in paper headlines, but it directly caps achievable speedup. For a given τ, the speedup is S ≈ τ/(1+γc). If c is large, even a perfect draft model (τ→∞) is bounded by a factor of 1/(γc). For EAGLE-3 with γ=16 and c=0.17, that ceiling is 1/(16×0.17) ≈ 0.37× — not a speedup at all if drafting cost dominated. In practice τ is finite and the actual speedup is much lower.</p>

<p>The Domino paper (2026) provides a rare empirical latency breakdown for three methods on A100 with Qwen3-8B and context length 1024:</p>

