<h3>Why DFlash's Drafting Is 7× Cheaper</h3>
<p>EAGLE-3 generates draft tokens autoregressively — producing γ=16 tokens requires 16 sequential forward passes through the draft Transformer. Each pass is a serial memory-bound operation that cannot be parallelized. DFlash instead uses a block diffusion model: all γ tokens are generated in a single parallel forward pass. Modern GPUs execute batched operations orders of magnitude more efficiently than equivalent serial operations, so DFlash's drafting cost is nearly constant in γ.</p>

<p>This changes the design space entirely. EAGLE-3 is forced to use very shallow architectures (single Transformer layer) to keep c manageable. DFlash can afford five draft layers — providing substantially higher draft quality — while still maintaining lower total latency than EAGLE-3's single-layer approach.</p>

<h3>Measuring c in Practice</h3>
<p>To profile c, run two timing experiments in your serving framework:</p>
<ol style="padding-left:1.5rem;list-style:decimal;font-size:.9rem;color:var(--muted2);line-height:1.8">
  <li>Time a single target-model forward pass (prefill + one verify step) → Tverify</li>
  <li>Time one complete drafting phase (producing γ tokens) with the draft model → Tdraft</li>
  <li>Compute c = Tdraft / Tverify</li>
</ol>
<p>Always profile at your actual deployment context length. Draft latency grows with context for autoregressive methods (KV-cache memory bandwidth increases) but is approximately constant for diffusion drafters.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 -->
<section id="s-recipe" data-code="recipe">
<div class="section-no">06 — The Recipe</div>
<h2>A Six-Step Protocol for Fair Benchmarking</h2>

<p>Given everything above, here is a concrete protocol for measuring speculative decoding in a way that produces reproducible, comparable results. Navigate through each step:</p>

<div class="step-list" id="step-list">
  <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Fix the hardware and backend</div>
      <div class="step-desc">All measurements in a comparison must use identical GPU model, driver version, and inference framework (SGLang, vLLM, Transformers, or TRT-LLM). Backend choice alone can shift speedup by 2–3×. Document CUDA version and batch size mode (continuous batching vs static).</div>
      <div class="step-code">gpu=A100-80GB  backend=SGLang-0.4.1  cuda=12.4</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Report τ as the primary draft-quality metric</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Mean acceptance length τ is backend-independent and isolates draft model quality from serving overhead. Always report it alongside speedup. Measure τ with a chain-structured draft (no tree), which is the cleanest signal. Use the per-position acceptance rates 0-α, 1-α, 2-α to detect error accumulation.</div>
      <div class="step-code">tau, alphas = measure_accept_length(model, draft, dataset, n=500)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Use at least three task categories</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Choose one math task (GSM8K or Math500), one code task (HumanEval or MBPP), and one conversational task (MT-Bench or Alpaca). This prevents selecting only math benchmarks where τ is highest. Report τ and speedup separately for each category — the average obscures the task sensitivity that users most need to know.</div>
      <div class="step-code">TASKS = ["gsm8k", "humaneval", "mt_bench"]  # minimum set</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Measure at temperature=0 and temperature=1</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Temperature=0 (greedy) gives the highest acceptance rates and is reproducible. Temperature=1 (sampling) is realistic for conversational use and shows a significant speedup drop. Both numbers matter. Methods that only report T=0 may be using acceptance conditions that don't apply at T=1.</div>
      <div class="step-code">for temp in [0, 1]:
    speedup = benchmark(model, draft, temp=temp)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="4">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Measure latency at batch=1 and throughput at batch≥32</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Single-request latency speedup (batch=1) answers: "does a user get their response faster?" Throughput at batch=32 or 64 answers: "does the serving cluster handle more requests per second?" These questions have different answers. Report both. If throughput drops below 1.0× at high batch, report that plainly — it means the method adds cost without benefit in that regime.</div>
      <div class="step-code">for batch in [1, 4, 16, 64]:
    lat = measure_latency(batch=batch)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" data-step="5">
    <div class="step-num">6</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Profile and report c (draft cost ratio)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The draft cost ratio c is the key architectural differentiator that papers rarely report. Measuring it takes 10 minutes with a simple profiling script and enables readers to predict speedup at their own γ and τ. Without c, a competitor cannot know whether to improve τ or reduce drafting cost next.</div>
      <div class="step-code">c = profile_draft_latency() / profile_verify_latency()</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

